{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-88b0ba0f02f63e07","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tepic_count: 7\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9014: Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability\n11\t\n12\tTriage (Step 0): not below threshold — the strategy spans three components\n13\t(Llama Stack, TrustyAI, Dashboard) and three owning teams, and contains a\n14\tgenuine unknown (the GA scope itself, per the Open Questions section) whose\n15\tresolution changes what the hardening work is. Not documentation-only — code\n16\tchanges are required in all three components. Full decomposition follows.\n17\t\n18\t## Epic List\n19\t\n20\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n21\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | GA-readiness assessment for Llama Stack | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | Close inventoried GA gaps in Llama Stack | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | API stability guarantee and tested upgrade path | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | Production support posture for Llama Stack GA | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | Supported TrustyAI guardrails integration for GA distributions | Implementation | TrustyAI | P1 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 | Dashboard Llama Stack management on by default for entitled clusters | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E007 | GA-standard documentation uplift with TP migration notes | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n29\t\n30\t## Dependency DAG\n31\t\n32\t```mermaid\n33\tgraph TD\n34\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 GA-readiness assessment]\n35\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 Close inventoried GA gaps]\n36\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 API stability and upgrade path]\n37\t    E004[RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 Production support posture]\n38\t    E005[RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 TrustyAI guardrails integration]\n39\t    E006[RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 Dashboard default enablement]\n40\t    E007[RHAISTRAT-9014-E007 GA documentation uplift]\n41\t    E001 --> E002\n42\t    E001 --> E003\n43\t    E003 --> E005\n44\t    E002 --> E007\n45\t    E003 --> E007\n46\t    E004 --> E007\n47\t    E005 --> E007\n48\t    E006 --> E007\n49\t```\n50\t\n51\tCritical path: E001 → E003 → E005 → E007 (length 4).\n52\t\n53\t## DAG Justification\n54\t\n55\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n56\t|------|------|-----------|\n57\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The GA-readiness assessment produces the definitive GA scope; the gap-remediation epic's very contents are the assessment's findings. True gate: `add_remediation` — the assessment may reveal problems requiring additional work. |\n58\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The strategy's open question states that \"the size of the stability and hardening work is an estimate\" until the assessment lands — the assessment's findings on API stability and upgrade paths can change this epic's scope. True gate: `rewrite` — scope/approach of the stability work adjusts to findings. The named HLR-2 deliverables (versioned APIs, deprecation policy, tested upgrade path) exist regardless, so the gate is `rewrite`, not `remove`. |\n59\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 12 (framework/library → consumer) | The supported TrustyAI guardrails integration is validated against and builds against Llama Stack's published API surface; it must target the versioned, stability-guaranteed surface rather than the TP-era surface it was experimentally wired against. |\n60\t| E002 → E007 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Docs describe what was built; the GA documentation must reflect the remediated component. |\n61\t| E003 → E007 | Rule 11 | GA documentation includes the API stability and deprecation policy and upgrade-path documentation. |\n62\t| E004 → E007 | Rule 11 | GA documentation includes SLO targets, backport policy, and the supported configuration matrix. |\n63\t| E005 → E007 | Rule 11 | GA documentation includes the supported guardrails integration. |\n64\t| E006 → E007 | Rule 11 | GA documentation reflects the default-on Dashboard enablement for entitled clusters. |\n65\t\n66\tEdges deliberately NOT drawn:\n67\t\n68\t- **E001 → E004 (not drawn)**: HLR-3's deliverables (SLO targets, backport policy, supported configuration matrix) are defined independent of the gap findings; the assessment does not determine whether this work exists. The coupling that does exist (the configuration matrix must reflect validated limits) is expressed as an acceptance-criterion gate on E004, not a DAG edge.\n69\t- **E001 → E005, E001 → E006 (not drawn)**: HLR-4 and HLR-5 define their deliverables fully; neither the TrustyAI integration support work nor the Dashboard default flip changes shape based on assessment findings.\n70\t- **No konflux/repo-onboarding edges**: all three components are already Konflux-onboarded per architecture context; no onboarding chain is generated (Rule 20 does not fire — every component has an architecture-context file).\n71\t- **E002 ∥ E004, E003 ∥ E004, E005 ∥ E006 etc. (parallel)**: implementations in different areas/repos with no build-against artifact flow between them run in parallel (Rules 14–15).\n72\t\n73\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n74\t\n75\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abbrev.) | Epic(s) |\n76\t|-----|----------|----------------------|---------|\n77\t| HLR-1 | P0 | GA-readiness assessment inventories gaps, produces definitive GA scope | E001 (assessment), E002 (gap closure) |\n78\t| HLR-2 | P0 | API stability guarantee: versioned APIs, deprecation policy, tested upgrade path | E003 |\n79\t| HLR-3 | P0 | Production support posture: SLOs, backport policy, config matrix | E004 |\n80\t| HLR-4 | P1 | TrustyAI guardrails as supported, documented integration | E005 |\n81\t| HLR-5 | P1 | Dashboard Llama Stack management on by default for entitled clusters | E006 |\n82\t| HLR-6 | P2 | Documentation uplifted to GA standards incl. TP migration notes | E007 |\n83\t\n84\tHLR-1 maps to two epics by work-product type: the investigation that produces\n85\tthe scope (E001) and the implementation that closes the inventoried gaps\n86\t(E002). All six HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements; no redundant or\n87\toverlapping HLR pairs were found (each HLR describes a distinct surface).\n88\t\n89\t## Health Warnings\n90\t\n91\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no formal Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements. Teams were inferred from the architecture-context owning teams (RHOAI GenAI, TrustyAI, RHOAI Dashboard); epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLR text. Non-blocking; human verification recommended.\n92\t- **Rule-driven priority note on E007**: HLR-6 is P2, but the docs-authoring priority exception derives the epic's priority from the strategy frontmatter (`priority: Critical` → P0). This is the rule-mandated outcome, not an inversion. Consequently a P0 epic is blocked by P1 epics (E005, E006) — an accepted consequence of the exception, which also exempts E007 from pushing priority upstream.\n93\t- **Assessment-size risk (recorded, not hidden)**: until E001 lands, E002's and E003's sizing is an estimate, per the strategy's own open question. This is recorded structurally via the `gated_by` / `gate_failure_impact` fields on both epics rather than as prose.\n94\t- **Rule 19 considered, not applied**: the strategy mentions guardrails (HLR-4), but the work is uplifting an existing, experimentally-flagged TrustyAI integration to supported status — not introducing new safety-critical mechanisms. Fail-mode validation of the guardrails integration is folded into E005's acceptance criteria instead of a standalone fail-mode Investigation.\n95\t- **Component span on E005**: HLR-4's work touches both the TrustyAI service side and the Llama Stack distribution wiring. The single most-relevant canonical component is `TrustyAI` (the integration's supported status is a TrustyAI-side deliverable owned by the TrustyAI team); the cross-component coordination with the Llama Stack distribution work is noted in that epic's scope.\n96\t- **Team for E007**: with no Impacted Teams table, the GA documentation epic is assigned to RHOAI GenAI (content owner for Llama Stack GA docs); if a dedicated documentation team owns this content, reassign before submission.\n97\t\n98\t## Tiered Delivery\n99\t\n100\tNot applicable — no external dependency with uncertain timing; all three\n101\tcomponents are in the platform (architecture context files exist for each),\n102\tso no upstream-PR gating or Tier 1/Tier 2 split is warranted.\n103\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-97bdaeacf2e590b1","agent_id":"a3bcbd58764a7d2ae","session_id":"39957edc-33e6-45f5-8209-165342cd81d6","uuid":"5e7a5ed8-7e3d-4ef8-bb3b-71137d9db39f","timestamp":"2026-10-26T16:17:31.249Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-88b0ba0f02f63e07","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9014 decomposition"}
