{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-889442642758cfd6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9014-E005\n3\ttitle: Supported TrustyAI guardrails integration for GA distributions\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n5\tcomponent: TrustyAI\n6\tteam: TrustyAI\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-9014-E003\n11\tai_signals:\n12\t  change_specificity: 0\n13\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n14\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n15\t  existing_foundation: 1\n16\t  open_questions: 1\n17\t  external_dependency: 0\n18\t  human_process_gates: 0\n19\t  repo_access: 1\n20\t  architecture_claims: 1\n21\timplementation_type: null\n22\tinvestigation_signals: null\n23\tai_implementability: High\n24\tai_implementability_score: 6\n25\tjira_key: null\n26\tbranch: null\n27\tgated_by: null\n28\tgate_failure_impact: null\n29\t---\n30\t# Supported TrustyAI guardrails integration for GA distributions\n31\t\n32\t## Description\n33\t\n34\tThis epic makes the TrustyAI safety-guardrails integration with Llama Stack\n35\tdistributions a supported, documented integration for GA — promoted from the\n36\texperimental flag it ships as today. The integration itself already exists\n37\texperimentally; this epic delivers the validation, testing, and documentation\n38\tthat \"supported\" requires, targeted at the versioned, stability-guaranteed GA\n39\tAPI surface.\n40\t\n41\t## Scope\n42\t\n43\t- Promote the guardrails integration from an experimental flag to a supported\n44\t  configuration path for GA Llama Stack distributions.\n45\t- Validate the integration against the versioned GA API surface (the\n46\t  stability-guaranteed surface, not the TP-era surface the experimental\n47\t  integration was wired against).\n48\t- Define and test the supported integration configurations (which TrustyAI\n49\t  service versions with which distribution configurations are supported).\n50\t- Validate and document fail-mode behavior: what happens to requests and\n51\t  distributions when the guardrails service is unavailable or degraded, and\n52\t  what the operator/administrator recovery path is.\n53\t- Document the integration for GA distributions: enablement, supported\n54\t  configurations, and failure behavior.\n55\t\n56\t## Acceptance Criteria\n57\t\n58\t- The guardrails integration is enabled through a supported configuration\n59\t  path — no experimental flag is required for GA distributions.\n60\t- The integration is validated by automated tests against the versioned GA\n61\t  API surface, and those tests pass.\n62\t- The supported integration configurations are enumerated and each is covered\n63\t  by a passing test.\n64\t- Fail-mode behavior of the integration (guardrails service unavailable or\n65\t  degraded) is tested and documented, including the recovery path.\n66\t- Integration documentation suitable for GA distributions is published\n67\t  (enablement, supported configurations, failure behavior).\n68\t\n69\t## HLR Traceability\n70\t\n71\t- **HLR-4 (P1)**: Safety guardrails via TrustyAI are a supported, documented\n72\t  integration (not an experimental flag) for GA distributions — this epic\n73\t  delivers that requirement in full.\n74\t\n75\t## Risks / Notes\n76\t\n77\t- This epic's work spans two components: the TrustyAI service side (owned by\n78\t  the TrustyAI team) and the Llama Stack distribution wiring; the single\n79\t  most-relevant component is TrustyAI, and cross-team coordination with the\n80\t  Llama Stack distribution work is required.\n81\t- The integration must build against the stability-guaranteed API surface, so\n82\t  this work serializes after the API stability work; starting earlier would\n83\t  validate against a surface that may still change.\n84\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-97bdaeacf2e590b1","agent_id":"a3bcbd58764a7d2ae","session_id":"39957edc-33e6-45f5-8209-165342cd81d6","uuid":"25980a51-73b6-4efc-9f44-4dd01e00339c","timestamp":"2026-10-