{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-88a263f16d09b81c","type":"tool_result","content":"124:10. `konflux-onboarding` ∥ general implementation parallel (config independent of code; AC gates first execution). **Add NO blocking edge in either direction.** It is tempting to make the general implementation depend on `konflux-onboarding` (\"the code needs a build pipeline first\") — do not. That coupling is enforced by the Rule 25 \"build pipeline green\" AC on the general implementation epic, never by a DAG dependency. A `konflux-onboarding → general` edge serializes work that must run in parallel and is a rule violation. This holds for EVERY `konflux-onboarding` × general-implementation pair in the decomposition, whatever the general epic's component or deliverable — a stack-wide assembly, packaging, or evidence epic that consumes the pipeline's outputs is still a general implementation. There is no \"different component\" or \"genuine artifact dependency\" exemption: if the coupling is real, express it as an AC on the consuming epic (\"the signed attestations from the build pipeline are included and validated\"), never as an edge.\n330:Add optional fields only when non-null: `implementation_type=<value>`, `branch=<value>`, `gated_by=<epic_id>`, `gate_failure_impact.action=<value> gate_failure_impact.fallback_approach=\"<text>\"`. Every epic that depends on an Investigation should have `gated_by` pointing to that Investigation (see Rule 3).\n349:It deterministically checks what downstream consumers enforce and an LLM step can silently get wrong: epic body sections (Description / Scope / Acceptance Criteria / HLR Traceability), `component` values verbatim in `.context/rhai-components.txt`, Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by every Implementation epic), priority inheritance (an epic blocking P0 work is itself P0), implementation-type parallelism (Rules 7/8/10 — no `dependencies` edge, in either direction, between `license-validation` and `repo-onboarding`, `license-validation` and `konflux-onboarding`, or `konflux-onboarding` and a general implementation; a flagged edge is removed from the dependent epic's `dependencies` and its coupling expressed as an AC gate instead), and shared-surface enforcement deliverables (a standalone enforcement-tool epic — lint rule, CI check, static-analysis check, validation harness — over surfaces implemented by same-component epics; the deliverable is divided per surface into the consuming epics' Scope/ACs and the standalone epic deleted). It prints one `VIOLATION:` line per problem and exits non-zero. Fix every violation by editing the epic files (never edit the script), re-run, and proceed only once it prints `OK`.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-97bdaeacf2e590b1","agent_id":"a3bcbd58764a7d2ae","session_id":"39957edc-33e6-45f5-8209-165342cd81d6","uuid":"eddcf300-8108-440d-a00b-ecb7de88b464","timestamp":"2026-10-26T16:19:43.897Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-88a263f16d09b81c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9014 decomposition"}
